    48	ABSTRACT
    49	
    50	
    51	Traditional call-graph construction algorithms conflate the computation of possible runtime types with the actual resolution of
    52	(virtual) calls. This tangled design impedes supporting complex language features and APIs and making systematic trade-offs between
    53	precision, soundness, and scalability. It also impedes implementation of precise downstream analyses that rely on type information.
    54	To address the problem, we propose Unimocg, a modular architecture for call-graph construction that decouples the computation
    55	of type information from resolving calls. Due to its modular design, Unimocg can combine a wide range of different call-graph
    56	algorithms with algorithm-agnostic modules to support individual
    57	language features. Moreover, these modules operate at the same
    58	precision as the chosen call-graph algorithm with no further effort.
    59	Additionally, Unimocg allows other analyses to easily reuse type
    60	information from the call-graph construction at full precision.
    61	We demonstrate how Unimocg enables a framework of call-graph
    62	algorithms with different precision, soundness, and scalability tradeoffs from reusable modules. Unimocg currently supports ten callgraph algorithms from vastly different families, such as CHA, RTA,
    63	XTA, and 푘 - 푙 -CFA. These algorithms show consistent soundness
    64	without sacrificing precision or performance. We also show how
    65	an immutability analysis is improved using Unimocg.
