   297	developers, on the other hand, can easily extend Unimocg to support new language features across all available algorithms or add
   298	new call-graph and/or other analysis algorithms while retaining all
   299	available feature support.
   300	As our evaluation shows, Unimocg’s benefits do not come at the
   301	cost of precision or performance compared to state-of-the-art static
   302	analysis frameworks.
   303	In summary, we make the following contributions:
   304	
   305	  - We analyze the reasons for inconsistent soundness in stateof-the-art call-graph algorithms (Section 2)
   306	
   307	  - We propose Unimocg, a novel architecture for implementing
   308	call-graph analyses modularly (Section 3). Unimocg allows
   309	deriving various call graphs with consistent soundness. In
   310	particular, we show how resolution of features like reflection
   311	can be decoupled from the call-graph algorithm.
   312	
   313	  - We discuss how Unimocg benefits analyses that depend on
   314	type information but do not participate in call-graph construction (Section 3.5).
   315	
   316	  - We use Unimocg to implement families of different call
   317	graphs and measure soundness, precision, and performance
   318	compared to the state of the art and evaluate their impact
   319	on a state-of-the-art immutability analysis (Section 4).
   320	
   321	The current implementation of Unimocg focuses on call-graph
   322	construction for JVM languages, but the addressed problems and
   323	the Unimocg architecture are language agnostic.
   324	
   325	
   326	2 PROBLEM STATEMENT
   327	
   328	
   329	We analyze two problems with existing call-graph algorithms responsible for the observed soundness inconsistency (Table 1).
   330	
   331	
   332	Problem 1: Coupling of Call Resolution of Language Features to
   333	Base Call-Graph Algorithms. Modern programming languages have
   334	many features, which are difficult to analyze. Three such Java features are reflection, (de)serialization, and threads. Reflection [ 12 ]
   335	dynamically instantiates classes and calls methods based on runtime strings and types. To resolve reflective calls, a call-graph analysis needs to statically determine strings for the class and method
   336	names. It also has to determine receiver and class objects as well
   337	as argument types. (De)serialization [ 22 ] writes or reads Java objects from a stream of bytes, e.g., a file. For (de)serialization, the
   338	JVM invokes special methods, e.g., `readResolve`, that must be handled by the call-graph analysis. To resolve a call on a deserialized
   339	object, a call-graph analysis also needs to determine the types
   340	of objects in the byte stream. Threads are started by calling the
   341	built-in `Thread.start` method, which leads to the JVM invoking
   342	`Thread.run` ; hence, it requires specific handling by call-graph algorithms. What complicates the problem further is that these features
   343	can be used in combination, e.g., threads may start `Runnable` objects loaded via reflection.
   344	Advanced features induce unique challenges for different callgraph algorithms—hence, each call-graph analysis typically treats
   345	them specifically. For example, reflection is easier to handle by
   346	call-graph algorithms that have allocation information and deserialization is easier for imprecise algorithms that over-approximate
   347	the possible classes deserialized.
   348	
   349	
   350	
   351	Handling language features differently creates coupling between
   352	call resolution of features and the base call-graph algorithms and
   353	violates separation of concerns. For example, WALA’s CHA does
   354	not use the call-graph-builder facilities of RTA and CFA but its
   355	own, redundant implementation of some features. Thus, WALA’s
   356	RTA algorithm handles static initializers differently from WALA’s
   357	CHA—with RTA, they should be deemed reachable only for classes
   358	actually instantiated. In Soot, the call-resolution code for RTA and
   359	Spark is strongly coupled to the call-graph builder class, which not
   360	only resolves standard calls, but is also responsible for recognizing
   361	reflection. Supporting new language features would thus require
   362	changing the call-graph builder class. Both Soot and Wala resolve
   363	only explicit calls. Features that are not explicit calls such as static
   364	initializers do not use the same call-resolution facilities.
   365	
   366	One might argue that this is only the result of missing care in
   367	the implementation and that more implementation effort could fix
   368	these inconsistencies. While true, without shared language feature
   369	support, this places a lot of burden onto developers of the callgraph analyses, both in terms of care and implementation effort to
